 The Ordinal Placement for each judge was based on Total Points awarded by that judge to the pairs, with the tiebreaker being Sporting Merit (2nd mark).  Final placement was determined by a Majority Placement rule.  Thus, if a pair were ranked first by a majority of the judges, that pair was placed first overall, and the process was repeated for each place.  Ties broken by a Subsequent Majority rule, i.e., if the pairs were ranked for the same position by the same number of judges, Majority Placement for the next higher position for each pair determined who was ranked higher.  The tiebreakers were then, in order, 1) Number of Majority Placements, 2) Total Ordinals of Majority, 3) Total Ordinals, 4) Total Points."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004546"} "Sydney Croll"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002135"} "Franz Wojtanowskyj"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002847"} "John Greig"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004605"} "Theo Klemm"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002269"} "Giovanni De Mori"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #6"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5001945"} "Émile Finsterwald"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #7"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002530"} "Howell Janes"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "As in the women’s event, the winner seemed pre-ordained. Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/82976"} "Barbara Wagner"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/96247"} "Bob Paul"] " had placed sixth at the 1956 Cortina Olympics, but had not been defeated since that time, winning the World Championships in 1957-59. They were an easy winner, being placed first by all seven judges. The silver medal went to the German European Champions, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81484"} "Marika Kilius"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/84536"} "Hans-Jürgen Bäumler"] ". The bronze medal went to the US husband-wife pair of " [:a {:href "/athletes/81712"} "Nancy"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/91930"} "Ron Ludington"] ". Bob Paul would later become well known as a choreographer for US skaters " [:a {:href "/athletes/81072"} "Peggy Fleming"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/81238"} "Dorothy Hamill"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/81087"} "Linda Fratianne"] " as well as American television stars Donny and Marie Osmond. Ron Ludington would also become a highly respected skating coach. The fourth-place finishers in Squaw Valley, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81394"} "Maria"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87508"} "Otto Jelinek"] ", were a brother-sister pair representing Canada. But they were Czech by birth, having fled that country in 1955 with their parents, and in 1962 they would win the World Championship held in their native Praha. The sixth-place finishers, " [:a {:href "/athletes/83118"} "Tatyana Zhuk"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/85984"} "Aleksandr Gavrilov"] ", would later coach several successful skaters, notably " [:a {:href "/athletes/82209"} "Irina Rodnina"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Majority Placements"] [:th {} "Total Ordinals of Majority"] [:th {} "Total Ordinals"] [:th {} "Total Points"] [:th {} "Reduced Points"] [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:th {} "Judge #6"] [:th {} "Judge #7"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/82976"} "Barbara Wagner"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/96247"} "Robert Paul"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]] [:td {} "7×1+"] [:td {} "7.0"] [:td {} "7.0"] [:td {} "80.4"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} "1.0 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81484"} "Marika Kilius"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/84536"} "Hans-Jürgen Bäumler"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "4×2+"] [:td {} "8.0"] [:td {} "1